# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The app makes no model calls. There is no Anthropic or Gemini key here any
# more, and nothing to add one for: every guide, style and step ships inside
# the bundle and is filtered on the device.
#
# What is left is one route — api/submissions.ts, which receives a style
# offered to the community library — and the settings it needs.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Comma-separated extra origins allowed to call the API, beyond the Capacitor
# defaults (https://localhost, capacitor://localhost, http://localhost:5173).
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase. The whole backend, and the only thing that needs provisioning.
#
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. One project serves all three things api/ needs:
#
#   - the moderation queue          (public.submissions)
#   - the photographs               (storage bucket "submission-photos")
#   - the rate-limit counters       (public.rate_counters + incr_rate_counter)
#
# Run supabase/migrations/0001_community_submissions.sql against a new project
# once — SQL Editor, or `supabase db push` — and copy the two values below out
# of Project Settings -> API.
#
# SERVER ONLY. The service role key bypasses row-level security entirely, so
# it is a full read/write credential for the whole database. It must never be
# given a VITE_ prefix: anything so prefixed is inlined into the bundle at
# build time and ships inside the APK, where it can be read straight out of
# the package. api/_supabase.ts throws on startup if it ever finds a
# VITE_-prefixed Supabase key, because that failure is otherwise silent.
#
# The device never talks to Supabase. It talks to api/submissions.ts, which is
# what keeps the validation, the rate limits and the "nothing auto-publishes"
# rule somewhere the client cannot route around. There is no anon key here
# because the client has no use for one.
#
# Leave both empty for local development: the route answers 503 and the device
# keeps every submission queued, which is the same thing it does when the
# server is simply down.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# The bucket photographs go in. Only set this if you renamed it; the default
# matches the migration.
SUPABASE_PHOTO_BUCKET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rate limiting (api/_limits.ts) — legacy Redis, optional.
#
# The counters live in Supabase now, so these are only read when SUPABASE_URL
# is unset. Kept working so a deployment already running on Vercel KV or
# Upstash does not break on this change; a new deployment should leave them
# empty and set the Supabase pair above.
#
# With neither set the limits are per-instance and the daily cap is not
# enforced at all, which is fine locally and not fine in production: the route
# does not authenticate its caller — a shared secret in the APK would be
# extractable, so it guards nothing — and these counters are the only thing
# between an open upload endpoint and whoever finds it. CORS does not help; it
# decides what a browser may read, and a script with curl never asks.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
KV_REST_API_URL=
KV_REST_API_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Client-side. Safe to expose — it is only a URL.
# The APK has no same-origin server, so it must be told where the deployed
# function lives. Leave empty for `npm run dev`.
#
# This value is inlined at build time. A release built with it empty still
# works everywhere except the "share a style" button, which is the one screen
# that needs a server. Put the deployed URL in `.env.production` (gitignored,
# loaded only by `vite build`) and leave this one empty for local dev:
#
#   # .env.production
#   VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://afro-coily-hair.vercel.app
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
VITE_API_BASE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Update checks. Client-side, safe to expose — it is a public URL.
#
# The landing site (`npm run site:build`) publishes latest.json beside the
# APK. Set this to its absolute URL and the release APK checks it on open
# and tells the user when a newer build has been posted (src/lib/updates.ts).
# Leave it empty and the build never checks; About says so.
#
# Inlined at build time, so like the API URL it belongs in .env.production
# before `npm run android:release`:
#
#   # .env.production
#   VITE_UPDATE_MANIFEST_URL=https://your-landing-site.example/latest.json
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
VITE_UPDATE_MANIFEST_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Community submissions (api/submissions.ts).
#
# The route refuses with a 503 unless both a durable record store and a photo
# store are configured — accepting a submission it cannot keep would tell the
# device it was sent and then lose it. Setting the Supabase pair above
# satisfies both at once; there is nothing else to configure.
#
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN is gone. It was for a Vercel Blob photo store that was
# never implemented — Supabase Storage is the photo store now, in the same
# project as the records. See docs/community-backend.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
